<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · c. 1170 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Can vei la lauzeta mover</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A love song from the 1100s by a troubadour, a poet-singer of the Middle Ages, who wishes he were as happy as a lark.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bernart de Ventadorn</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Bernart de Ventadorn</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the troubadours of Occitania</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>12th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and melody by the troubadour Bernart de Ventadorn (12th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Can vei la lauzeta mover&quot; is a song in the Occitan language by Bernart de Ventadorn, a troubadour of the 12th century. It is among the oldest and the best known of all troubadour songs. Both its words and its melody survive, in variants from three different <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. Its lauzeta is a lark.</p><p>The song is a first-person poem of seven stanzas of eight lines each, with a closing coda of four lines. It has been called one of the first poems to dramatise the effect of someone actually speaking in the present.</p><p>It travelled beyond the Occitan-speaking south. In the 13th century it was one of three Occitan songs set into the French romance Guillaume de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a>, and it is one of two similar insertions in Gerbert de Montreuil's Le roman de la violette. Some scholars have suggested that it inspired a tercet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a> (Paradiso XX:73–75), which also describes a lark in flight; others think Dante may have met the image through the poet Bondie Dietaiuti, or that a lark's flight was too common a sight for any link to be drawn. In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> Pound translated parts of the song in Canto 6 of The Cantos and returned to its image in Canto 117.</p><p>The song is still performed, and it has been recorded on many albums.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of unrequited love. The singer watches a lark rise joyfully towards the sun and fall, and wishes he could feel such joy, but his love for a woman who does not return it keeps him from it. He compares himself to figures of legend who were undone by love and resolves to go into exile if she will not love him. The mood is longing and bitter.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
</div>
